{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785307154597,"sessionID":"ses_053835f44ffewNv42EZbxtppsV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_D9pB7Nmo8APoxFB7TWfajNzn","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/iterations/iteration-003.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/iterations/iteration-003.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Iteration 3: Automation gaps and lifecycle ownership\n2: \n3: ## Focus\n4: Dimension 3 only: identify where scaffolding, generation, freshness, reconciliation, and lifecycle ownership stop short across the eight governed skill-root JSON types plus advisor graph/index and compiled-route projections. The selected interpretation treats human routing meaning (`edges`, modes, aliases, intent vocabulary, command choreography, serving authority) as authored policy, while class boilerplate, repeated identities, corpus inventories, derived labels, and consumer-specific projections are automation candidates. Effectiveness and test-depth judgments remain deferred to iterations 4 and 5.\n5: \n6: ## Findings\n7: 1. **Highest leverage — creation does not produce a contract-complete skill root.** The standalone path writes `SKILL.md`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `leaf-manifest.config.json`, then tells the operator only to edit prose, add resources, and run an unspecified validator; it never invokes the fleet gate that creates required `leaf-manifest.json` and S-class `leaf-aliases.json`. The parent path similarly writes the five authored H JSON surfaces and an empty command surface but no leaf manifest; only compiled routing has an optional mint/freshness branch. Because both classes require a generated manifest and S additionally requires generated aliases, `init_skill.py` hands off a structurally incomplete root and leaves lifecycle ownership to human memory. The scaffold should finish by running the canonical generated-artifact producer/check, without inventing authored policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:321-340] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:583-665] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:69-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:323-357]\n8: \n9: 2. **Highest leverage — `graph-metadata.json.derived` has a capable writer but no production owner, and new scaffolds emit a third, incompatible shape.** Both scaffold branches hand-author `trigger_phrases`, `key_topics`, `source_docs`, and legacy timestamps. The TypeScript sync instead derives and atomically writes a provenance-fingerprinted `keywords` schema, while the Python compiler requires non-empty `key_topics`, `key_files`, `entities`, `source_docs`, `causal_summary`, and legacy timestamps. A scoped production-code search found `syncDerivedMetadata` exported only from its module and invoked only by tests/stress tests; the live watcher responds to changes by reindexing, not by calling sync. Thus there is neither one canonical generated schema nor an end-to-end trigger that reconciles it before indexing. Human-owned `intent_signals`, domains, and edges should remain untouched; the whole `derived` block needs one schema/producer and an explicit create/change/rebuild owner. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:270-294] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:515-558] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts:35-55] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts:92-145] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:294-325] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts:425-426] [INFERENCE: the scoped `syncDerivedMetadata` search returned only the defining module and test/stress invocations]\n10: \n11: 3. **High leverage — the fleet gate and CI stop before advisor ingestibility, so authored graph policy and generated derived data lack one reconciliation/freshness boundary.** The root gate parses graph metadata only to detect nested skill-shaped identities; `checkRoot()` then automates manifest/alias freshness and H command metadata but never validates graph schema, edge targets, paths, or derived shape. The Python compiler does validate domains, intent signals, edge targets, derived paths, entities, and timestamps, yet the routing drift workflow runs only the root and leaf-manifest gates. A root can therefore be green at the class/freshness boundary while failing the downstream advisor compiler. The missing owner is a non-mutating end-to-end gate (and a separate explicit repair path), not automatic rewriting of human edge weights or intent policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:98-140] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:305-357] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:270-303] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:306-396] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n12: \n13: 4. **Medium-high leverage — required standalone config is mostly conventional boilerplate and should become defaults-plus-overrides.** The scaffold repeats `workflowMode = skill_name`, `packet = \".\"`, `excludeIndexFiles = true`, and the contract version for every S root. The generator already defaults packet, the common `references`/`assets` roots, index exclusion, and contract version. Only deviations such as additional package roots or a non-default packet are human choices. Generating the conventional config (or allowing it to be absent when conventions suffice) while retaining an authored override file would remove mandatory synchronization without erasing policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:295-308] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:95-127] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:82-87]\n14: \n15: 5. **Medium leverage — H scaffolding seeds cross-file projections but has no post-edit reconciliation owner.** One initializer repeats hub/packet/mode identities across `mode-registry.json`, nested `advisorRouting`, `hub-router.json`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `description.json`; its own next step asks the operator to rename/replace the example mode, after which only validation is suggested. The semantic mode choice, weighted vocabulary, aliases, tool policy, graph relationships, description prose, and command choreography are intentional human policy. In contrast, repeated skill/version/mode/packet identifiers, nested packet identity, singleton tie-breaks, and empty command arrays are projections that can be generated or cross-file-reconciled from a declared authority. The existing fleet gate validates command metadata against registry modes and disk, but it does not reconcile the broader seeded identity set. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:453-572] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:667-672] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs:7-34] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:254-302] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:125-142]\n16: \n17: 6. **Medium leverage — compiled-route artifact mechanics are automated, but source-change-to-refresh ownership is not fleet-integrated.** The compiler consumes exactly `SKILL.md`, `hub-router.json`, and `mode-registry.json`; mint creates a canonical generation-1 manifest, while refresh recompiles one generation ahead, preserves serving authority/shadow state, takes a writer lease, and publishes atomically. That is the correct generated/projection boundary. However, parent scaffolding runs mint/freshness only at creation when explicitly requested, and the routing drift CI block runs the root/leaf gates without compiled-route freshness. The gap is orchestration: registry/router changes need a fleet freshness signal and an explicit safe refresh owner, while `servingAuthority` and `shadowOnly` must remain operator-controlled policy rather than regenerated defaults. [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:384-435] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:600-637] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:650-753] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:618-665] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n18: \n19: ## Ruled Out\n20: - **Using spec-folder `generate-description.js` or `backfill-graph-metadata.js` to repair skill-root identity.** The fleet gate explicitly excludes the spec tree because same-named files there use a separate continuity schema; sharing a filename is not shared lifecycle ownership. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:21-26]\n21: - **Generating all H JSON from packet folders.** Mode semantics, router weighting, compatibility relocation, graph relationships, command intent/choreography, and activation authority are not inferable boilerplate; the root contract permits unattended writes only for class-specific generated manifest/alias projections. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:125-152]\n22: - **Keeping the output-first freshness scanner as a second authoritative lifecycle gate.** It can byte-check committed manifests but cannot see missing adoption; the SKILL-root fleet gate already owns that complete discovery boundary. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs:54-84] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:74-86]\n23: \n24: ## Dead Ends\n25: - No generator can safely infer authored routing vocabulary, edge meaning/weights, compatibility relocations, command choreography, or serving-authority flips from directory structure alone. Automation should project and reconcile identities around those declarations, not replace them.\n26: \n27: ## Edge Cases\n28: - Ambiguous input: the named spec-folder description/graph generators share filenames with skill metadata but own a different schema; they were classified as an intentional boundary, not reused.\n29: - Contradictory evidence: three shapes currently claim ownership of schema-v2 `derived` data (scaffold legacy shape, TypeScript sync shape, Python compiler shape); the conflict remains unresolved and blocks declaring any one producer canonical.\n30: - Missing dependencies: none required; local source established the lifecycle paths.\n31: - Partial success: one broad repository grep returned sibling-lineage matches despite the lineage read restriction. Those matches were discarded, are not cited, and did not inform the findings; all evidence above comes from production/config sources or this lineage's prior state.\n32: \n33: ## Sources Consulted\n34: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:270-672`\n35: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:95-240`\n36: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:74-412`\n37: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs:54-132`\n38: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:44-152`\n39: - `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs:7-120`\n40: - `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts:35-55`\n41: - `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts:92-145`\n42: - `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:270-396`\n43: - `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts:190-262,361-640`\n44: - `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/freshness.ts:90-189`\n45: - `.opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:384-753`\n46: - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110`\n47: - `.opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml:138-150`\n48: \n49: ## Assessment\n50: - New information ratio: 0.83\n51: - Questions addressed: Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\n52: - Questions answered: The automation question is answered with six ranked lifecycle gaps and explicit human-policy boundaries.\n53: \n54: ## Reflection\n55: - What worked and why: Following each scaffolded file through generation, class validation, advisor indexing, and compiled activation exposed ownership handoffs that filename inventories alone conceal.\n56: - What did not work and why: A broad repository grep crossed into sibling lineage output; its scope was too wide, so those results were excluded and subsequent searches were constrained to production directories.\n57: - What I would do differently: Start with an explicit allowlisted set of production roots for every grep, then build the lifecycle matrix from create → edit → validate → generate → ingest → refresh.\n58: \n59: ## Recommended Next Focus\n60: Dimension 4 only: measure which authored and derived metadata actually contributes to advisor candidate selection, lane attribution, hub-mode routing, and fallbacks; distinguish root selection effectiveness from post-selection compiled routing.\n\n(End of file - total 60 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Iteration 3: Automation gaps and lifecycle ownership\n\n## Focus\nDimension 3 only: identify where scaffolding, generation, freshness, reconciliation, and lifecycle ownership stop short across the eight governed skill-root JSON types plus advisor graph/index and compiled-route projections. The selected interpretation treats human routing meaning (`edges`, modes, aliases, intent vocabulary, command choreography, serving authority) as authored policy, while class boilerplate, repeated identities, corpus inventories, derived labels, and consumer-specific projections are automation candidates. Effectiveness and test-depth judgments remain deferred to iterations 4 and 5.\n\n## Findings\n1. **Highest leverage — creation does not produce a contract-complete skill root.** The standalone path writes `SKILL.md`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `leaf-manifest.config.json`, then tells the operator only to edit prose, add resources, and run an unspecified validator; it never invokes the fleet gate that creates required `leaf-manifest.json` and S-class `leaf-aliases.json`. The parent path similarly writes the five authored H JSON surfaces and an empty command surface but no leaf manifest; only compiled routing has an optional mint/freshness branch. Because both classes require a generated manifest and S additionally requires generated aliases, `init_skill.py` hands off a structurally incomplete root and leaves lifecycle ownership to human memory. The scaffold should finish by running the canonical generated-artifact producer/check, without inventing authored policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:321-340] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:583-665] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:69-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:323-357]\n\n2. **Highest leverage — `graph-metadata.json.derived` has a capable writer but no production owner, and new scaffolds emit a third, incompatible shape.** Both scaffold branches hand-author `trigger_phrases`, `key_topics`, `source_docs`, and legacy timestamps. The TypeScript sync instead derives and atomically writes a provenance-fingerprinted `keywords` schema, while the Python compiler requires non-empty `key_topics`, `key_files`, `entities`, `source_docs`, `causal_summary`, and legacy timestamps. A scoped production-code search found `syncDerivedMetadata` exported only from its module and invoked only by tests/stress tests; the live watcher responds to changes by reindexing, not by calling sync. Thus there is neither one canonical generated schema nor an end-to-end trigger that reconciles it before indexing. Human-owned `intent_signals`, domains, and edges should remain untouched; the whole `derived` block needs one schema/producer and an explicit create/change/rebuild owner. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:270-294] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:515-558] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts:35-55] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts:92-145] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:294-325] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts:425-426] [INFERENCE: the scoped `syncDerivedMetadata` search returned only the defining module and test/stress invocations]\n\n3. **High leverage — the fleet gate and CI stop before advisor ingestibility, so authored graph policy and generated derived data lack one reconciliation/freshness boundary.** The root gate parses graph metadata only to detect nested skill-shaped identities; `checkRoot()` then automates manifest/alias freshness and H command metadata but never validates graph schema, edge targets, paths, or derived shape. The Python compiler does validate domains, intent signals, edge targets, derived paths, entities, and timestamps, yet the routing drift workflow runs only the root and leaf-manifest gates. A root can therefore be green at the class/freshness boundary while failing the downstream advisor compiler. The missing owner is a non-mutating end-to-end gate (and a separate explicit repair path), not automatic rewriting of human edge weights or intent policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:98-140] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:305-357] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:270-303] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:306-396] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n\n4. **Medium-high leverage — required standalone config is mostly conventional boilerplate and should become defaults-plus-overrides.** The scaffold repeats `workflowMode = skill_name`, `packet = \".\"`, `excludeIndexFiles = true`, and the contract version for every S root. The generator already defaults packet, the common `references`/`assets` roots, index exclusion, and contract version. Only deviations such as additional package roots or a non-default packet are human choices. Generating the conventional config (or allowing it to be absent when conventions suffice) while retaining an authored override file would remove mandatory synchronization without erasing policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:295-308] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:95-127] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:82-87]\n\n5. **Medium leverage — H scaffolding seeds cross-file projections but has no post-edit reconciliation owner.** One initializer repeats hub/packet/mode identities across `mode-registry.json`, nested `advisorRouting`, `hub-router.json`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `description.json`; its own next step asks the operator to rename/replace the example mode, after which only validation is suggested. The semantic mode choice, weighted vocabulary, aliases, tool policy, graph relationships, description prose, and command choreography are intentional human policy. In contrast, repeated skill/version/mode/packet identifiers, nested packet identity, singleton tie-breaks, and empty command arrays are projections that can be generated or cross-file-reconciled from a declared authority. The existing fleet gate validates command metadata against registry modes and disk, but it does not reconcile the broader seeded identity set. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:453-572] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:667-672] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs:7-34] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:254-302] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:125-142]\n\n6. **Medium leverage — compiled-route artifact mechanics are automated, but source-change-to-refresh ownership is not fleet-integrated.** The compiler consumes exactly `SKILL.md`, `hub-router.json`, and `mode-registry.json`; mint creates a canonical generation-1 manifest, while refresh recompiles one generation ahead, preserves serving authority/shadow state, takes a writer lease, and publishes atomically. That is the correct generated/projection boundary. However, parent scaffolding runs mint/freshness only at creation when explicitly requested, and the routing drift CI block runs the root/leaf gates without compiled-route freshness. The gap is orchestration: registry/router changes need a fleet freshness signal and an explicit safe refresh owner, while `servingAuthority` and `shadowOnly` must remain operator-controlled policy rather than regenerated defaults. [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:384-435] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:600-637] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:650-753] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:618-665] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n\n## Ruled Out\n- **Using spec-folder `generate-description.js` or `backfill-graph-metadata.js` to repair skill-root identity.** The fleet gate explicitly excludes the spec tree because same-named files there use a separate continuity schema; sharing a filename is not shared lifecycle ownership. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:21-26]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/iterations/iteration-003.md","text":"# Iteration 3: Automation gaps and lifecycle ownership\n\n## Focus\nDimension 3 only: identify where scaffolding, generation, freshness, reconciliation, and lifecycle ownership stop short across the eight governed skill-root JSON types plus advisor graph/index and compiled-route projections. The selected interpretation treats human routing meaning (`edges`, modes, aliases, intent vocabulary, command choreography, serving authority) as authored policy, while class boilerplate, repeated identities, corpus inventories, derived labels, and consumer-specific projections are automation candidates. Effectiveness and test-depth judgments remain deferred to iterations 4 and 5.\n\n## Findings\n1. **Highest leverage — creation does not produce a contract-complete skill root.** The standalone path writes `SKILL.md`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `leaf-manifest.config.json`, then tells the operator only to edit prose, add resources, and run an unspecified validator; it never invokes the fleet gate that creates required `leaf-manifest.json` and S-class `leaf-aliases.json`. The parent path similarly writes the five authored H JSON surfaces and an empty command surface but no leaf manifest; only compiled routing has an optional mint/freshness branch. Because both classes require a generated manifest and S additionally requires generated aliases, `init_skill.py` hands off a structurally incomplete root and leaves lifecycle ownership to human memory. The scaffold should finish by running the canonical generated-artifact producer/check, without inventing authored policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:321-340] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:583-665] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:69-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:323-357]\n\n2. **Highest leverage — `graph-metadata.json.derived` has a capable writer but no production owner, and new scaffolds emit a third, incompatible shape.** Both scaffold branches hand-author `trigger_phrases`, `key_topics`, `source_docs`, and legacy timestamps. The TypeScript sync instead derives and atomically writes a provenance-fingerprinted `keywords` schema, while the Python compiler requires non-empty `key_topics`, `key_files`, `entities`, `source_docs`, `causal_summary`, and legacy timestamps. A scoped production-code search found `syncDerivedMetadata` exported only from its module and invoked only by tests/stress tests; the live watcher responds to changes by reindexing, not by calling sync. Thus there is neither one canonical generated schema nor an end-to-end trigger that reconciles it before indexing. Human-owned `intent_signals`, domains, and edges should remain untouched; the whole `derived` block needs one schema/producer and an explicit create/change/rebuild owner. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:270-294] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:515-558] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts:35-55] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts:92-145] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:294-325] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts:425-426] [INFERENCE: the scoped `syncDerivedMetadata` search returned only the defining module and test/stress invocations]\n\n3. **High leverage — the fleet gate and CI stop before advisor ingestibility, so authored graph policy and generated derived data lack one reconciliation/freshness boundary.** The root gate parses graph metadata only to detect nested skill-shaped identities; `checkRoot()` then automates manifest/alias freshness and H command metadata but never validates graph schema, edge targets, paths, or derived shape. The Python compiler does validate domains, intent signals, edge targets, derived paths, entities, and timestamps, yet the routing drift workflow runs only the root and leaf-manifest gates. A root can therefore be green at the class/freshness boundary while failing the downstream advisor compiler. The missing owner is a non-mutating end-to-end gate (and a separate explicit repair path), not automatic rewriting of human edge weights or intent policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:98-140] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:305-357] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:270-303] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:306-396] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n\n4. **Medium-high leverage — required standalone config is mostly conventional boilerplate and should become defaults-plus-overrides.** The scaffold repeats `workflowMode = skill_name`, `packet = \".\"`, `excludeIndexFiles = true`, and the contract version for every S root. The generator already defaults packet, the common `references`/`assets` roots, index exclusion, and contract version. Only deviations such as additional package roots or a non-default packet are human choices. Generating the conventional config (or allowing it to be absent when conventions suffice) while retaining an authored override file would remove mandatory synchronization without erasing policy. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:295-308] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:95-127] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:82-87]\n\n5. **Medium leverage — H scaffolding seeds cross-file projections but has no post-edit reconciliation owner.** One initializer repeats hub/packet/mode identities across `mode-registry.json`, nested `advisorRouting`, `hub-router.json`, `graph-metadata.json`, and `description.json`; its own next step asks the operator to rename/replace the example mode, after which only validation is suggested. The semantic mode choice, weighted vocabulary, aliases, tool policy, graph relationships, description prose, and command choreography are intentional human policy. In contrast, repeated skill/version/mode/packet identifiers, nested packet identity, singleton tie-breaks, and empty command arrays are projections that can be generated or cross-file-reconciled from a declared authority. The existing fleet gate validates command metadata against registry modes and disk, but it does not reconcile the broader seeded identity set. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:453-572] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:667-672] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs:7-34] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:254-302] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:125-142]\n\n6. **Medium leverage — compiled-route artifact mechanics are automated, but source-change-to-refresh ownership is not fleet-integrated.** The compiler consumes exactly `SKILL.md`, `hub-router.json`, and `mode-registry.json`; mint creates a canonical generation-1 manifest, while refresh recompiles one generation ahead, preserves serving authority/shadow state, takes a writer lease, and publishes atomically. That is the correct generated/projection boundary. However, parent scaffolding runs mint/freshness only at creation when explicitly requested, and the routing drift CI block runs the root/leaf gates without compiled-route freshness. The gap is orchestration: registry/router changes need a fleet freshness signal and an explicit safe refresh owner, while `servingAuthority` and `shadowOnly` must remain operator-controlled policy rather than regenerated defaults. [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:384-435] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:600-637] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:650-753] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:618-665] [SOURCE: .github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110]\n\n## Ruled Out\n- **Using spec-folder `generate-description.js` or `backfill-graph-metadata.js` to repair skill-root identity.** The fleet gate explicitly excludes the spec tree because same-named files there use a separate continuity schema; sharing a filename is not shared lifecycle ownership. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:21-26]\n- **Generating all H JSON from packet folders.** Mode semantics, router weighting, compatibility relocation, graph relationships, command intent/choreography, and activation authority are not inferable boilerplate; the root contract permits unattended writes only for class-specific generated manifest/alias projections. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:125-152]\n- **Keeping the output-first freshness scanner as a second authoritative lifecycle gate.** It can byte-check committed manifests but cannot see missing adoption; the SKILL-root fleet gate already owns that complete discovery boundary. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs:54-84] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:74-86]\n\n## Dead Ends\n- No generator can safely infer authored routing vocabulary, edge meaning/weights, compatibility relocations, command choreography, or serving-authority flips from directory structure alone. Automation should project and reconcile identities around those declarations, not replace them.\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Ambiguous input: the named spec-folder description/graph generators share filenames with skill metadata but own a different schema; they were classified as an intentional boundary, not reused.\n- Contradictory evidence: three shapes currently claim ownership of schema-v2 `derived` data (scaffold legacy shape, TypeScript sync shape, Python compiler shape); the conflict remains unresolved and blocks declaring any one producer canonical.\n- Missing dependencies: none required; local source established the lifecycle paths.\n- Partial success: one broad repository grep returned sibling-lineage matches despite the lineage read restriction. Those matches were discarded, are not cited, and did not inform the findings; all evidence above comes from production/config sources or this lineage's prior state.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:270-672`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs:95-240`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs:74-412`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs:54-132`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs:44-152`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs:7-120`\n- `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts:35-55`\n- `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts:92-145`\n- `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py:270-396`\n- `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts:190-262,361-640`\n- `.opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/freshness.ts:90-189`\n- `.opencode/bin/lib/compiled-route-manifest.cjs:384-753`\n- `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml:109-110`\n- `.opencode/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml:138-150`\n\n## Assessment\n- New information ratio: 0.83\n- Questions addressed: Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\n- Questions answered: The automation question is answered with six ranked lifecycle gaps and explicit human-policy boundaries.\n\n## Reflection\n- What worked and why: Following each scaffolded file through generation, class validation, advisor indexing, and compiled activation exposed ownership handoffs that filename inventories alone conceal.\n- What did not work and why: A broad repository grep crossed into sibling lineage output; its scope was too wide, so those results were excluded and subsequent searches were constrained to production directories.\n- What I would do differently: Start with an explicit allowlisted set of production roots for every grep, then build the lifecycle matrix from create → edit → validate → generate → ingest → refresh.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\nDimension 4 only: measure which authored and derived metadata actually contributes to advisor candidate selection, lane attribution, hub-mode routing, and fallbacks; distinguish root selection effectiveness from post-selection compiled routing.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":60,"totalLines":60,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/iterations/iteration-003.md","time":{"start":1785307154577,"end":1785307154593}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_09e79cb1d083c918016a69a012631c819193bd783a9cf24ffd"}},"id":"prt_fac99488c001Ewwq1dlDdcSsXx","sessionID":"ses_053835f44ffewNv42EZbxtppsV","messageID":"msg_fac9903f00013jUdRzvWbUW5Z6"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785307154597,"sessionID":"ses_053835f44ffewNv42EZbxtppsV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_n8fFU94WC7JO0asepHHi0pf7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/deltas/iter-003.jsonl"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-doc/019-skill-routing-refactor/029-skill-json-optimization-research/research/lineages/sol-high/deltas/iter-003.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":3,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"run\":3,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Dimension 3: automation gaps across scaffolding, generated projections, freshness/reconciliation, and end-to-end lifecycle ownership\",\"findingsCount\":6,\"newInfoRatio\":0.83,\"noveltyJustification\":\"4 of 6 findings were fully new and 2 partially extended prior scaffold/derived observations: (4 + 0.5 * 2) / 6 = 0.83 rounded.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Use spec-folder description/graph generators for skill-root identity\",\"Generate semantic H routing policy from packet folders\",\"Keep the output-first manifest scanner as a second authoritative lifecycle gate\"],\"focusTrack\":\"automation-lifecycle\",\"edgeCase\":\"partial-success\",\"toolsUsed\":[\"memory_match_triggers\",\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Grep\",\"Glob\",\"Bash\",\"apply_patch\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-serve... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n2: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f1\",\"rank\":\"highest\",\"summary\":\"Scaffolds stop before producing class-required generated manifests and standalone aliases, leaving new roots contract-incomplete.\"}\n3: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f2\",\"rank\":\"highest\",\"summary\":\"Derived graph metadata has conflicting scaffold, TypeScript-sync, and Python-compiler schemas, while the sync writer has no production lifecycle trigger.\"}\n4: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f3\",\"rank\":\"high\",\"summary\":\"The fleet/CI boundary validates root class projections but not downstream graph ingestibility, edge/path integrity, or derived freshness.\"}\n5: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f4\",\"rank\":\"medium-high\",\"summary\":\"Standalone manifest config is mostly conventional boilerplate and can become generated defaults plus authored overrides.\"}\n6: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f5\",\"rank\":\"medium\",\"summary\":\"Hub scaffolding repeats derivable identities across five authored JSON surfaces without post-edit reconciliation; semantic policy must remain authored.\"}\n7: {\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f6\",\"rank\":\"medium\",\"summary\":\"Compiled-route mint/refresh is safe and atomic, but no fleet source-change-to-freshness/refresh owner connects it to registry and router edits.\"}\n8: {\"type\":\"ruled_out\",\"direction\":\"Use spec-folder description/graph generators for skill-root identity\",\"reason\":\"Same filenames carry a separate continuity schema.\"}\n9: {\"type\":\"ruled_out\",\"direction\":\"Generate semantic H routing policy from packet folders\",\"reason\":\"Vocabulary, edges, aliases, command choreography, and serving authority require human intent.\"}\n\n(End of file - total 9 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":3,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"run\":3,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Dimension 3: automation gaps across scaffolding, generated projections, freshness/reconciliation, and end-to-end lifecycle ownership\",\"findingsCount\":6,\"newInfoRatio\":0.83,\"noveltyJustification\":\"4 of 6 findings were fully new and 2 partially extended prior scaffold/derived observations: (4 + 0.5 * 2) / 6 = 0.83 rounded.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which hand-authored JSON and validation steps could be generated, scaffolded, or automatically checked?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Use spec-folder description/graph generators for skill-root identity\",\"Generate semantic H routing policy from packet folders\",\"Keep the output-first manifest scanner as a second authoritative lifecycle gate\"],\"focusTrack\":\"automation-lifecycle\",\"edgeCase\":\"partial-success\",\"toolsUsed\":[\"memory_match_triggers\",\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Grep\",\"Glob\",\"Bash\",\"apply_patch\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/generate-leaf-manifest.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-skill-root-metadata.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/ci-leaf-manifest-freshness.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/skill-root-metadata-contract.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/sk-doc/create-skill/scripts/lib/command-metadata-schema.cjs\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/schemas/skill-derived-v2.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/derived/sync.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/scripts/skill_graph_compiler.py\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-server/lib/daemon/watcher.ts\",\".opencode/skills/system-skill-advisor/mcp-serve... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f1\",\"rank\":\"highest\",\"summary\":\"Scaffolds stop before producing class-required generated manifests and standalone aliases, leaving new roots contract-incomplete.\"}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f2\",\"rank\":\"highest\",\"summary\":\"Derived graph metadata has conflicting scaffold, TypeScript-sync, and Python-compiler schemas, while the sync writer has no production lifecycle trigger.\"}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f3\",\"rank\":\"high\",\"summary\":\"The fleet/CI boundary validates root class projections but not downstream graph ingestibility, edge/path integrity, or derived freshness.\"}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f4\",\"rank\":\"medium-high\",\"summary\":\"Standalone manifest config is mostly conventional boilerplate and can become generated defaults plus authored overrides.\"}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f5\",\"rank\":\"medium\",\"summary\":\"Hub scaffolding repeats derivable identities across five authored JSON surfaces without post-edit reconciliation; semantic policy must remain authored.\"}\n{\"type\":\"finding\",\"id\":\"iter-003-f6\",\"rank\":\"medium\",\"summary\":\"Compiled-route mint/refresh is